TRACING SOCKET / SELECT / PIPE BEHAVIOR

The trace recorder can show syscall entries and exits for socket,
read, write, select, accept and other operations supported by the
current kernel. Not every underlying lwIP action is a syscall.

INVESTIGATION
  ps
  lsof
  trace on -s
  ip
  trace show
  trace on
For an actual socket workload, start the trace on one console,
perform a bounded network request from another, then inspect it.
Do not start a permanent server through MCP and wait for it.

READING A SELECT TRACE
  select entry: application asked to wait for readiness.
  select/ exit: inspect return count; 0 may mean timeout, a
  positive value means descriptor readiness, negative means error.
  Repeated immediate select returns may indicate a polling loop,
  but only compare with timeout and FD set behavior if known.
  A read after select may still return a short count or EOF.

COMMON MISINTERPRETATIONS
  - A successful accept does not mean a complete HTTP request.
  - A TCP write returning N does not guarantee peer processed data.
  - SWITCH does not prove what kernel function caused blocking.
  - A missing event may have been overwritten or disabled.
  - Kernel symbols are not stack traces; correlate addresses only
    with the matching firmware build.

If MCP reports incomplete chunked read, first verify the actual
state (ps, socket listeners, prior write effects). Do not blindly
repeat any state-changing operation.
